.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt IMP-EXPORTS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imp-exports
.Nd write the declarations of the routines and variables of IMP72 sources for C, Zig and Rust
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c | z | r
.Op Fl n Ar name
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Ar source ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads IMP72 sources as text, the comments and the strings skipped, and writes the
declarations that a program in another language needs to call the routines they define
and to use the variables they declare
.Sy COMMON .
A
.Sy SUBR
is a function whose parameters are pointers to 64-bit words and whose result is a word, so
.Ql SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B
is
.Ql int64_t ADD2(int64_t *A, int64_t *B); :
the parameters stay by reference, which is what they are.
A
.Sy COMMON
variable is
.Ql extern int64_t X; ,
and one declared with a length
.Pq Ql A IS COMMON,8 LONG
an array,
.Ql extern int64_t A[8]; .
A routine with no parameters
.Pq Ql NIL
is declared with none.
The names are as written: IMP72 names are case sensitive and a symbol is its name.
A parameter named like a keyword of the language written for gets an underscore after it.
.Pp
The declarations of a type are not written: IMP72 has no typed export, and the object's
symbols say nothing of the parameters.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c
A C header, with an include guard and
.Ql extern "C"
for C++ (the default).
.It Fl z
A Zig file:
.Ql pub extern fn ADD2(A: *i64, B: *i64) i64;
and
.Ql pub extern var X: i64; .
.It Fl r
A Rust file: an
.Ql extern "C"
block with
.Ql pub fn ADD2(A: *mut i64, B: *mut i64) -> i64;
and
.Ql pub static mut X: i64; .
.It Fl n Ar name
The name of the header, which names the guard of a C header; by default the base name of the first source.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the file (the standard output by default).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 for a source that cannot be read or an unknown option; 2 for the usage.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr imp 1 ,
.Xr imp-cimport 1
